# Design Job Board: build spec

## Goal
A bare-bones, minimalist public job board for **design roles only** (product design, UX, UI/visual, brand, design systems, UX research, content design, motion, design engineering, design leadership). What sets it apart is **precise seniority filtering** (nine levels instead of LinkedIn's lumped "Mid-Senior") and **freshness** (no listing stays up longer than 30 days).

## Look and feel
- Minimal and calm, in the spirit of Claude's UI, with a tasteful expressive share. Follow 60/30/10: about 60% canvas (`bg`), 30% structure (surfaces, ink, muted text, borders, roman serif headings), and 10% accent (one clay hue, italic serif, the discipline ticker, seniority badges).
- Light and dark mode. The first visit follows the system preference. A header control stores an explicit choice. Components use semantic tokens, so both themes share one set of class names.
- Headings use Newsreader. Body text uses Source Sans 3.
- Use subtle 1px borders and soft radius, with no heavy shadows or gradients.
- It should be fast, responsive, and accessible (keyboard-navigable filters, visible focus states, good contrast in both themes).
- Tokens live in `app/globals.css`. The contract lives in `lib/design-tokens.ts`. Primitives live in `components/ui`. The gallery is `/design`. The write-up is `DESIGN_SYSTEM.md`. `npm test` fails if those drift or if a component introduces a raw color.

## Stack
- Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind, deployed on Vercel.
- Postgres (e.g. Neon via the Vercel Marketplace) for listings. Provide a local fallback (SQLite or local Postgres) so it runs without cloud credentials.
- A Vercel Cron job runs once daily for ingestion and cleanup. The route is protected by `CRON_SECRET`.

## Pages
1. **Landing (`/`)**
   - A hero with a one-line pitch.
   - Value props: **"We keep jobs fresh"**, **"Only 30 days allowed on here"**, and **"Better filters to find roles"** (nine real seniority levels, years of experience, industry).
   - A call to action to browse jobs, plus an optional preview of the latest listings.
2. **Jobs (`/jobs`)**
   - A filter sidebar (a drawer on mobile) with a result count and a "Clear filters" control.
   - Filter state lives in URL query params so views are shareable.
   - Each job card shows the title, company, location or remote status, seniority badge, years range, industry tag, discipline tags, salary (when available), and posted age ("3d ago").
   - The **Apply** button links directly to the company's original posting.
3. **Optional:** a simple job detail view and a short About/FAQ explaining the 30-day rule and how seniority is decided.

## Data sources
Use the public, no-auth ATS job-board APIs:
- **Greenhouse:** `https://boards-api.greenhouse.io/v1/boards/{token}/jobs?content=true`
- **Ashby:** `https://api.ashbyhq.com/posting-api/job-board/{name}?includeCompensation=true`
- **Lever (optional):** `https://api.lever.co/v0/postings/{company}?mode=json`

Keep a curated, easy-to-edit companies config (e.g. `data/companies.ts`) with these fields:
`name`, `ats` (`greenhouse` | `ashby` | `lever`), `token`, `industry`, `sizeBucket` (e.g. `1-50`, `51-200`, `201-1000`, `1000+`), `website`.

Seed it with 50+ design-forward companies across industries. Verify that each token returns data, and drop the ones that don't.

Ingestion:
- Fetch every company once a day.
- Keep only design roles, using a title include/exclude keyword list. Exclude engineering senses of "design", such as mechanical, chip/ASIC, hardware, and circuit design engineer.
- Upsert by a stable key of `source + company + jobId`.
- Store: title, company, URL, location, remote type, employment type, salary min/max/currency (when present), description (plain text), source posted/updated date, `first_seen_at`, `last_seen_at`, and the derived fields below.

## 30-day freshness rule
- A listing's age is measured from the source posted date if one exists, otherwise from `first_seen_at`.
- Listings **older than 30 days are deleted.**
- Listings that **disappear from the source feed are deleted.**
- Both cleanups run in the daily cron job right after ingestion.

## Seniority classification (the core feature)
Each listing gets exactly one level. The classifier combines **title signals** with **years of experience** parsed from the description.

| Level | Title signals | Typical years |
|---|---|---|
| New grad | New Grad, University, Graduate, Early Career, 2026/2027 grad | 0 |
| Entry level | Associate, Junior, Designer I, Apprentice | 0–2 |
| Mid-senior | Plain Product Designer, Designer II, UX Designer | 2–5 |
| Senior | Senior, Sr., Designer III | 5–8 |
| Staff | Staff, some uses of Lead | 8+ |
| Principal | Principal, Distinguished | 10+ |
| Manager | Design Manager, Manager, Product Design, Lead + direct reports | 6+ with people management |
| Director | Director, Sr. Director, Head of Design (larger company) | 10+ |
| Executive | VP, SVP, Chief Design Officer, Head of Design (startup) | 12+ |

Edge cases:
- **"Lead"** is treated as an individual-contributor level (Staff) unless the description mentions people management ("direct reports", "hire", "grow a team", "manage a team of designers"). With those signals it becomes Manager.
- **"Head of Design"** uses company size: a startup (roughly under 200 people) means Executive, and a larger company means Director.
- **Title vs. years conflict:** keep the title's level and add a **"Stretch"** badge (e.g. a "Senior" title asking for 3+ years).
- **No years stated** (about a third of listings): fall back to the title alone.
- Leave a pluggable hook for an optional cheap LLM pass on truly ambiguous listings. It sits behind an env var and is **off by default**, so the rules must work on their own.

### Years-of-experience parser
- Extract `yearsMin` / `yearsMax` from phrases like `5+ years`, `3-5 years`, `3–5 years`, `3 to 5 years`, `at least 4 years`, `minimum of 6 years`, and spelled-out numbers (`five years`).
- Ignore unrelated numbers (e.g. "5 years of runway", company age).
- Write unit tests for both the parser and the classifier using realistic title and description samples.

## Filters and tags
Filters are multi-select where it makes sense.
- **Seniority level:** the nine levels above.
- **Years of experience:** a min/max range, with a toggle to include listings that state no years. This should support queries like "Senior roles asking for 5 years or fewer."
- **Industry:** fintech, defense/govtech, AI/ML, healthcare, climate/energy, dev tools, consumer social, e-commerce/marketplace, enterprise SaaS, productivity, crypto/web3, gaming, media/entertainment, education, mobility/transportation, hardware/robotics, security, travel/hospitality. Industry comes mainly from the company config.
- **Discipline:** product design, UX, UI/visual, brand, design systems, UX research, content design, motion, design engineering, design leadership. Derived from the title and description.
- **Work type:** remote / hybrid / onsite, plus a location or country text search.
- **Employment type:** full-time / contract / internship.
- **Salary:** show it when provided, with a "Has salary" filter.
- **Company size** bucket.
- **Posted within:** 24h / 3d / 7d / 14d / 30d.
- **Keyword search** across title and company.
- **Sort:** newest (default) or company A–Z.
- **Badges:** level, Stretch, Remote, and Salary listed.

## Environment variables
- `DATABASE_URL` is the Postgres connection string.
- `CRON_SECRET` protects the daily cron route.
- `LLM_CLASSIFIER_ENABLED` is optional and defaults to `false`. A matching API key is needed only if it's enabled.

## Done when
- The app runs locally, a real ingest against the live APIs fills the database, and every filter works end to end.
- The daily cron route (ingest, then 30-day and stale deletion) works and is protected.
- The classifier and parser tests pass.
- The README covers local setup, adding companies, env vars, and deploying to Vercel (including the cron config in `vercel.json`).
